Module 6 – Storage
Lesson 4 – Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)

Amazon S3 (Simple Storage Service) is a fully managed object storage service that allows you to store and retrieve virtually unlimited amounts of data in the AWS Cloud. It is designed for storing unstructured data such as images, videos, documents, backups, logs, and application files.

What is Amazon S3?

Amazon S3 stores data as objects.

Instead of storing files in folders like a traditional file system, S3 stores files as objects inside containers called buckets.

Bucket
│
├── image.jpg
├── video.mp4
├── backup.zip
└── document.pdf
S3 Objects

An S3 object is the fundamental unit of storage in Amazon S3.

Each object contains:

Actual data (file)
Metadata
Unique identifier (Key)

Objects can store:

Images
Videos
Documents
Application data
Backup files

Object size:

Few bytes minimum
Up to 5 TB maximum per object
S3 Buckets

A bucket is a container used to store objects.

Features:

Globally unique bucket name
Can store virtually unlimited objects
Created in a specific AWS Region
Supports versioning, logging, and access control

Example:

Bucket Name:
anuja-portfolio-images

Objects:
logo.png
profile.jpg
resume.pdf
Key Features of Amazon S3
Virtually Unlimited Storage

There is no practical limit on the amount of data stored in S3.

You can store:

Thousands of files
Millions of files
Petabytes of data
Highly Durable Storage

Amazon S3 provides:

99.999999999%

(11 Nines) durability.

AWS automatically stores multiple copies of objects across infrastructure within a Region.

This protects data from loss.

Versioning

Versioning protects files from:

Accidental deletion
Accidental modification

Example:

Version 1
Resume.pdf

Version 2
Resume.pdf

Version 3
Resume.pdf

Previous versions can be restored.

Managed Service

AWS handles:

Infrastructure
Scaling
Availability
Replication

Users only manage their data.

Common Use Cases
Website Hosting

Store website files such as:

HTML
CSS
JavaScript
Images
Backup Storage

Store backups of:

Applications
Databases
Files
Media Storage

Store:

Images
Videos
Audio files
Data Archiving

Store long-term business data.

Data Lakes

Store large amounts of structured and unstructured data for analytics.

Example: Ecommerce Website

An online shopping website stores:

Product Images
Product Videos
Documents

inside Amazon S3.

When customers open the website, the images are retrieved directly from S3.

Even if traffic suddenly increases, S3 automatically scales.

Security in Amazon S3

Everything stored in S3 is:

PRIVATE BY DEFAULT

Only the object owner can access it until permissions are granted.

Bucket Policies

Bucket Policies control:

Who can access data
What actions they can perform

Example:

Allow Public Read Access

or

Allow Only Company Employees
Identity-Based Policies (IAM Policies)

Permissions can also be controlled using IAM users, groups, and roles.

Example:

Developer
→ Upload Files

Manager
→ View Files

Admin
→ Full Access
Encryption

Amazon S3 supports encryption for security.

Encryption at Rest

Protects data stored inside S3 buckets.

Encryption in Transit

Protects data while moving between:

User
↓
Internet
↓
Amazon S3
Additional Security Features
Presigned URLs

Provide temporary access to objects.

Example:

Valid for 1 Hour

After expiry, access is automatically removed.

S3 Access Points

Simplify access management for shared datasets.

S3 Audit Logs

Track:

Who accessed data
When data was accessed
What actions were performed
Benefits of Amazon S3
Scalability

Stores virtually unlimited data.

Durability

11 Nines durability protects data.

Cost Optimization

Supports lifecycle policies and storage classes.

Security

Private by default with multiple security controls.

Broad Use Cases

Suitable for websites, backups, archives, media storage, and analytics.




// Lesson 5 – Amazon S3 Storage Classes and S3 Lifecycle

Amazon S3 provides multiple storage classes so you can store data based on how often it is accessed. Frequently accessed data uses one storage class, while rarely accessed or archived data uses another. This helps optimize storage costs while maintaining the required level of availability and performance.

Why S3 Storage Classes?

Not all data is accessed equally.

Example:

New Product Images → Accessed Daily
1-Year-Old Backups → Rarely Accessed
10-Year Compliance Records → Almost Never Accessed

Storing all data in the same storage class would be expensive.

AWS provides different storage classes to reduce costs.

Amazon S3 Storage Classes
1. S3 Standard

Default storage class.

Features
Frequently accessed data
Low latency
High throughput
Multi-AZ storage
High availability
Use Cases
Dynamic websites
Mobile applications
Gaming applications
Big Data analytics
Frequently used files
Example
Website Images
Product Photos
Application Files
2. S3 Standard-IA

(Infrequent Access)

Used when data is accessed less frequently but must be available immediately when needed.

Features
Lower storage cost than Standard
Fast retrieval
Multi-AZ durability
Use Cases
Backups
Disaster recovery files
Older project files
Example
Monthly Backup Files
Old Customer Reports
3. S3 Intelligent-Tiering

Automatically moves objects between storage tiers based on usage patterns.

Features
Automatic optimization
No need to manually manage lifecycle rules
Cost-efficient
Use Cases
Data lakes
Unknown access patterns
Large datasets
Example
Company Documents
Research Data

AWS automatically decides the best tier.

4. S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval

For archived data that still requires immediate access.

Features
Lower cost
Millisecond retrieval
Archive storage
Use Cases
Medical images
Media archives
Historical data
Example
X-Ray Images
Old Video Content
5. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval

For archive data that is rarely accessed.

Features
Lower storage cost
Retrieval time:
Minutes
Hours
Use Cases
Long-term backups
Archive storage
Example
Old Company Backups
Archived Documents
6. S3 Glacier Deep Archive

Lowest-cost storage class.

Features
Very low storage cost
Retrieval within about 12 hours
Use Cases
Compliance records
Legal documents
Long-term preservation
Example
10-Year Tax Records
Government Archives
7. S3 One Zone-IA

Stores data in a single Availability Zone.

Features
Lower cost
Less resilience than Multi-AZ options
Use Cases
Easily recreated data
Secondary backups
8. S3 Express One Zone

Provides extremely fast access in a single Availability Zone.

Use Cases
Performance-sensitive applications
High-speed workloads
Storage Class Comparison
Storage Class	Access Frequency
S3 Standard	Frequently Accessed
Standard-IA	Occasionally Accessed
Intelligent-Tiering	Unknown Access Pattern
Glacier Instant Retrieval	Rare Access but Immediate Retrieval
Glacier Flexible Retrieval	Rare Access
Glacier Deep Archive	Almost Never Accessed
One Zone-IA	Infrequent Access (Single AZ)
Express One Zone	High-Speed Access
S3 Lifecycle

S3 Lifecycle automatically manages data throughout its lifecycle.

Instead of manually moving files between storage classes, AWS does it automatically.

Lifecycle Actions
1. Transition Action

Moves objects to another storage class.

Example:

Day 0
↓
S3 Standard

Day 30
↓
S3 Standard-IA

Day 90
↓
S3 Glacier

Day 365
↓
S3 Glacier Deep Archive
2. Expiration Action

Deletes objects automatically after a defined period.

Example:

Log Files
↓
Store for 90 Days
↓
Automatically Delete
Example Lifecycle Policy

Imagine social media posts.

New Post
↓
Frequently Accessed

After 30 Days
↓
Less Accessed

After 1 Year
↓
Archive

After 5 Years
↓
Delete

Lifecycle policies automate this process.

Benefits of S3 Lifecycle
Cost Optimization

Automatically moves data to cheaper storage.

Automation

No manual management required.

Compliance

Retains data for required periods.

Storage Efficiency

Deletes unnecessary data automatically.

Real-Life Example

A company stores:

Customer Logs

Access Pattern:

First 30 Days → Frequently Used

Next 60 Days → Occasionally Used

After 90 Days → Rarely Used

Lifecycle Policy:

Day 0 → S3 Standard

Day 30 → Standard-IA

Day 90 → Glacier

Later → Deep Archive

Eventually → Delete

This significantly reduces storage costs.

Lesson Summary
S3 Standard

Frequently accessed data

S3 Standard-IA

Infrequently accessed data

S3 Intelligent-Tiering

Automatically optimizes storage costs

Glacier Instant Retrieval

Archive + Immediate access

Glacier Flexible Retrieval

Archive + Minutes/Hours retrieval

Glacier Deep Archive

Lowest-cost archive storage

S3 Lifecycle
